// ── string utilities that must survive the representation change ─────────────
//
// STAGE 2c MADE `s[i]` A BYTE AND `.length` A BYTE COUNT. Two idioms in this tree read
// FALSE after that change, and both fail SILENTLY — they produce a valid string, just the
// wrong one, because a byte and a code point are both `i32` and nothing in the type system
// separates them (`docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Char-literal trap names exactly this):
//
//   1. `while i < s.length { buf.push(s[i]) } ; fromCodePoints(buf)`
//      `fromCodePoints`'s contract is fixed by its name — each `i32` is a CODE POINT — so
//      feeding it bytes RE-ENCODES each byte as its own character. `"é"` (C3 A9) comes back
//      as `"Ã©"`. This is `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §Builders' defect, found in
//      `std/str.vl` before the swap and found in ELEVEN places in `compiler/*.vl` during it.
//
//   2. `s.length` used as a WIDTH — a column, a pad, a "how many characters" — rather than
//      as an OFFSET handed back to `slice`. Offsets cancel: whatever unit produced them is
//      the unit that consumes them. Widths do not, so they have to say which unit they mean.
//
// This module is the one place each answer is written. It is a LEAF (no imports), so every
// module in the graph can reach it.
//
// EVERY FUNCTION HERE IS UNIT-STABLE — it gives the same answer under the pre-2c
// code-point representation and the post-2c byte one — and that is not a nicety. The
// compiler that builds the next compiler is itself built by the previous one, so this exact
// source runs under BOTH representations on the bootstrap ladder. A helper that answered
// differently on the two rungs would make `compile(X) == X` stop converging.

// Append `s`'s CODE POINTS to a `fromCodePoints` buffer.
//
// `for cp in s` yields code points under both representations — Stage 2c changed its
// LOWERING (it decodes UTF-8 now) and deliberately not its surface — so producer and
// consumer are a matched pair whose meanings move together, i.e. not at all.
export function pushCps(buf: i32[], s: string) {
  for cp in s { buf.push(cp) }
}

// The CODE POINT count of `s` — `.length` when a WIDTH is meant rather than an offset.
// O(n) and deliberately not spelled as a property, per §Codepoints ("operations whose cost
// depends on the data get a name, not a subscript").
//
// It does NOT call the core `s.cpLen()`, even though that is the same number in O(n) with
// no decode call per point. `cpLen` is a Stage 2c intrinsic, and the seed that compiles
// this source on the bootstrap rung predates it — a compiler that uses a method its own
// predecessor cannot lower does not build. Reach for `cpLen()` in `std/` and in user code,
// where the only compiler that ever sees it is the current one.
export function cpCount(s: string) {
  let n = 0
  for cp in s { n = n + 1 }
  n
}
